## D4: audit immutability (psql, app role)
$ psql -tAc "UPDATE audit_events SET action='tampered' WHERE action='auth.login.succeeded' RETURNING 'should-not-appear';"
ERROR:  milpbooklm: audit_events is immutable
CONTEXT:  PL/pgSQL function milpbooklm_immutability_violation() line 3 at RAISE
update_exit=1
$ psql -tAc "DELETE FROM audit_events WHERE action='auth.login.failed' RETURNING 'should-not-appear';"
ERROR:  milpbooklm: audit_events is immutable
CONTEXT:  PL/pgSQL function milpbooklm_immutability_violation() line 3 at RAISE
delete_exit=1
$ psql -tAc "SELECT count(*) FROM audit_events;"
4   (row count unchanged - the attempted tamper was rejected)

SMOKE_RESULT: PASS (C1-C10, A1-A11, D1-D4 all as expected; no process left running; scratch PG stopped)

Operational notes:
- Two real defects were caught by this smoke and fixed before commit (see
  smoke_caught_defects in task-7-milpbookml-implementation.json):
  1. store() IntegrityError-fallback ran in the already-aborted transaction
     (InFailedSqlTransaction) -> replaced with one atomic ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE
     upsert (the unique index is NULLS NOT DISTINCT, so NULL-owner rows conflict).
  2. PyNaCl Aead.encrypt returns an EncryptedMessage (bytes subclass) whose
     content is nonce || ciphertext; the first store persisted the nonce-prefixed
     blob and every subsequent decrypt failed AEAD authentication -> the cipher
     now persists only the ciphertext (the nonce lives in its own column).
- The smoke's first credential pass (before defect 2's fix) produced the
  "credential AEAD authentication failed" failure mode - which independently
  demonstrated the fail-closed decryption path on corrupted-in-format data.
- Engine DSNs must be postgresql+psycopg:// (make_engine maps plain
  postgresql:// to psycopg2, which is not installed) - FND-04/06 learning re-confirmed.

## Follow-up correction smoke (2026-09-20)

- W3C validation: repeated non-zero trace/span IDs were accepted and echoed; an all-zero trace ID was rejected.
- Structured logging: message, nested extras, and exception traceback text were scrubbed. Pre-installed package and descendant handlers produced no duplicate output after configuration; the root host handler remained untouched and received no application record.
- Keyring boundary: a POSIX keyring with group-read permission exited non-zero before parsing with an actionable owner-only-permissions message that did not disclose its path; mode 0600 succeeded.
- Error baseline: a real TestClient request to a raising endpoint returned uniform JSON 500 with the effective request ID, valid traceparent, CSP, nosniff, referrer, and permissions headers. Exception detail was absent from response and logs.
- Credential audit: against a fresh migrated PostgreSQL 17 scratch database, initial store plus same-scope update produced two `credential.stored` rows; one completed k1-to-k2 rotation produced one `credential.rotated` row. The credential ended on k2, and audit JSON contained none of the prohibited secret-content keys.
- Gates: Ruff passed; strict mypy passed (95 files); import-linter kept 4/4 contracts (130 files, 570 dependencies); existing meta/architecture/unit suite passed (127 tests, 2 dependency deprecation warnings).
- Teardown: scratch PostgreSQL stopped and all temporary keyring/secret/database files removed.

